WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.677 --> 00:00:02.554
Wearing an "I Voted" sticker on election day

00:00:02.554 --> 00:00:06.772
announces that you are a proud participant in the grand tradition of representative democracy,

00:00:06.772 --> 00:00:10.680
the worst system, except all the others. It says, "I care."

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.571
When Americans are dropping their ballots in mailboxes, standing in line, 

00:00:13.571 --> 00:00:17.171
pushing buttons, pulling levers, filling bubbles, or poking a touchscreen, 

00:00:17.171 --> 00:00:20.500
there are three little words that provide a surefire way to start a fight.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:21.780
I don't vote.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:27.000
The reasons people give for why they vote and why everyone else should too are flawed, unconvincing,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.472
and occasionally dangerous. The case for voting relies on factual errors, 

00:00:31.472 --> 00:00:33.370
misunderstandings about the duties of citizenship,

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.570
and overinflated perceptions of self-worth.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.500
There are some good reasons for some people to vote some of the time,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.170
but there are a lot more bad reasons to vote, and the bad ones are more popular.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:48.350
Your vote is wildly unlikely to determine the outcome of an election. It's pure math.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:53.040
In all of American history, a presidential election has never come down to a single vote.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.270
The chance of that happening is just one in 60 million.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:02.100
In a couple of key states, the probability that a random vote will be decisive creeps closer to one in 10 million,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.958
which is still like gunning for the Mega-Lotto jackpot. 

00:01:04.958 --> 00:01:08.980
In the 20th century,  just one congressional race was determined by a single vote.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:10.970
In Buffalo, New York in 1910.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.930
So are people who vote irrational, evil, or stupid?

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.700
Not necessarily. At least not all of those things.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.220
"The next time a friend of yours tells you he's not voting, don't try to change his mind,"

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.960
Harvard Economist Gregory Mankiw wrote a few years ago in a column that was originally slated for Forbes,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.264
but that his editors refuse to publish.

00:01:27.264 --> 00:01:31.806
Mankiw's argument drew on the work of Economist Timothy Feddersen and Wolfgang Pesendorfer

00:01:31.806 --> 00:01:38.200
who cited the phenomenon of "roll-off," or people who make it all the way inside the polyester curtains on election day,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.230
but then leave some ballot sections blank.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:45.330
They were illustrating the point that people who believe themselves ill-informed routinely choose not to vote.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.920
This increases the quality of voters who actually pull the lever for one side or the other.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.880
Older, better educated people are much more likely to vote,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.532
which also suggests that the pool of voters is better informed

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:58.970
and more qualified to make election-related judgments  than the pool of non-voters.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.700
If it's true that "education makes people better voters," Mankiw writes,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.490
 it would make sense for the "less educated" to "show up at the polls less often."

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.660
But people aren't particularly good at knowing whether or not they're well-informed.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.330
Many people who closely follow politics hold views that are dangerous and wrong,

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.760
and no one knows what a politician will do after arriving in Washington.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.696
The gap between the promised and real consequences of electing one guy over the other

00:02:21.696 --> 00:02:23.910
is very difficult to anticipate. 

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.370
Rock the vote.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.370
There's a case to be made that many people have a civic duty not to vote.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.970
Boosting turnout among citizens who are young, uneducated, or otherwise less likely to be engaged

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.830
has the unintended consequence of encouraging people to fail in that duty.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.420
It's true even though no particular vote is likely to be decisive.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:47.420
None of this is to suggest that the government should in any way use legal means to limit the people who can vote.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.200
Voting is a fundamental right and should never be abridged.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.020
This is about an individual's moral choice about whether or not to vote.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.130
Jason Brennan conjures this terrifying thought experiment.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.920
Imagine you come across a firing squad about to kill an innocent child.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.950
Assume all the bullets will strike at the same time and there's nothing you can do to stop them.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:10.640
You are invited to be the 101st member of the squad. It's still immoral to shoot, Brendan argues,

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.550
based on the principle that "one should not participate in collectively harmful activities

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.157
when the cost of refraining from such activities is low."

00:03:17.157 --> 00:03:22.270
If you believe your vote is likely to be ill-informed  or that a particular race is likely to yield an unfair,

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:26.200
unjust, or otherwise bad outcome, you should refrain from participating.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.530
Get out the vote campaigns, promote precisely the kind of morally condemnable ignorant voting

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.223
we should be discouraging.

00:03:32.223 --> 00:03:37.340
This is the perspective that informs those "Don't Vote, It Only Encourages the Bastards" bumper stickers.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.284
Washing one's hands of the whole system is a good way to ensure that they remain clean,

00:03:41.284 --> 00:03:43.782
even when the politicos are dirty. 

00:03:43.782 --> 00:03:46.100
What if everybody stopped voting?

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:50.310
What if the arguments against voting were so persuasive that everyone stopped voting?

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:55.358
This worry, which channels the categorical imperative of the 18th Century German Philosopher, Immanuel Kant,

00:03:55.358 --> 00:03:59.203
posits that if everybody behaved as non-voters do, the whole system would fall apart.

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:03.696
A certain minimum level of participation is necessary for elections to appear legitimate.

00:04:03.696 --> 00:04:09.000
This objection is natural and intuitive, but the fact is that one person's decision not to vote

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.114
or even to make a video about not voting,

00:04:11.114 --> 00:04:15.366
is unlikely to substantially influence the tens of millions of people who already vote. 

00:04:15.366 --> 00:04:21.000
If you like, you could say the rule is don't vote unless your vote is likely to substantially influence

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.540
the outcome of the election.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:28.070
If someone found herself in an electorate with zero other voters, she could happily write in her own name, for instance,

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.740
without violating the general anti-voting principle.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.363
If someone invests in an enterprise, we generally recognize that he has more of a right than an outsider

00:04:35.363 --> 00:04:37.338
to determine the course of that enterprise. 

00:04:37.338 --> 00:04:40.010
But sitting out an an election is also an affirmative choice.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:45.482
As the English radical, Herbert Spencer, pointed out in 1851, if a voter picks a losing candidate,

00:04:45.482 --> 00:04:48.010
the argument goes that by taking part in such an election,

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:50.920
he tacitly agreed to abide by the decision of the majority,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.940
but if he abstains, he can not justly complain, seeing as he made no protest.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:05:00.030
Curiously enough, Spencer writes, it seems that he gave his consent in whatever way he acted,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:06.620
whether he said yes, whether he said no, or whether he remained neuter, a rather awkward doctrine, this, indeed.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:11.613
Complaining is another way to make good on our civic duty to be engaged, if such a thing exists,

00:05:11.613 --> 00:05:14.300
and it's mercifully unrelated to our duty to vote.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:18.133
It may be that people don't vote though because of what they think their vote can accomplish,

00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:20.912
but instead, for what it says about who they are.

00:05:20.912 --> 00:05:22.929
Voting is the cheapest form of altruism.

00:05:22.929 --> 00:05:28.420
Since there's little in the way of material payoff, voting maximizes your halo rather than your bottom line.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:32.651
Voting sociotropically, Philosopher Jason Brennan writes, is cheaper and easier

00:05:32.651 --> 00:05:35.490
than volunteering at a soup kitchen, or giving money to Oxfam.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:40.380
A 2009 survey found that 88% of respondents, all of whom were academics,

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.420
considered voting in public elections to be morally good.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.850
They considered voting to be on par with regularly donating blood and giving 10% of one's income to charity.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:53.970
The philosophers, Lemanski and Brennan, theorized that voting is best understood as an expressive act.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.510
Communicating preferences at the ballot box is something people do for its own sake,

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.670
not a duty they perform or a selfish bid for material gain.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:03.280
Decked out in team colors, cheering as loudly as you can,

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.110
the chance that your individual voice will sway the outcome of a game is vanishingly small,

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.730
but you are communicating to the other people at the game,

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:16.300
I am one of you. I value the system in which we each participate. I am loyal.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.490
The economist, Bryan Caplan, takes the idea a step further.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:24.460
Perhaps, he suggests, voting is more like cheering while watching the same game from your recliner

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.080
in a darkened living room.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:32.820
If you really try, you can still tell an ultimately unsatisfying story about why your actions matter in the rest of the world.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:38.500
After all, your viewership of the game might show up in television ratings, which boost the team's advertising revenue.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.820
Of course, you're probably not a Nielsen household,

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:44.290
so you may not show up in any of the metrics of the teams owners can see,

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.680
which leaves the solitary game watchers right there with voters.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.610
The main payoff is that you can say you did it.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:51.840
So what's wrong with that?

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.808
Individual cases of expressive voting in large elections are just as unlikely to affect the outcome of the election

00:06:56.808 --> 00:06:58.046
as other kinds of voting.

00:06:58.046 --> 00:07:02.680
But the fact of widespread and expressive voting explains why elections are silly seasons.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.270
Politicians offer themselves up as opportunities for expressive voting,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:11.720
as aggregations of easily comprehensible slogans rather than as avatars of sensible policy.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:17.030
Ignorant expressive voters, even rationally ignorant ones, may be committing immoral acts.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:21.008
All of which is a pretty steep price to pay for an "I Voted" sticker.

